I've added the change and committed it as 78e45157. When most of the sessions this check reads can't be checked, the trajectory now says the instrument itself is the cause and names the two settings that collide.

First real run, against the actual audit-log sessions:
```
Shape, not a per-run accident: 6 of 10 claiming sessions open before the clone. Window = last 10 sessions; clone = 55 commits, oldest 2026-09-29 20:32 UTC; 3 of the 10 sessions read open their window after it. The window is this script's; the fetch depth is set in .github/workflows/evolve.yml (protected).
```
The 55-commit figure matches git rev-list --count HEAD.

What changed, in `scripts/extract_trajectory.py`:
• **When it fires:** the new line `clone_window_mismatch_line` is added to the block only when sessions refused for starting before the clone are at least half of the sessions that claimed success.
• **Numbers are measured, not typed:** the window size, the clone's commit count, the oldest commit's date, and how many sessions start after that date. If git fails, the line says the count "could NOT be checked" rather than printing a number. The oldest-commit date is the same value the refusals were decided against.
• **Nothing else moved:** WINDOW_SESSIONS and every existing count and verdict are unchanged.
• **Self-tests:**
  • The majority case is built with the real classifier and must render the exact expected line, including through render_outcomes.
  • The git-failure case must use the "could NOT be checked" wording.
  • When refusals are a minority (Day 214's 2 of 5 case) or there are none, the output must match today's exactly.
• **Positive control:** I forced the threshold to always fire, marked `NEUTERED`, then ran and restored it in one command. Exactly the three majority-case tests failed by name. The file is restored with no marker left.

`ARCHITECTURE.md`: a Day-215 entry under the extract_trajectory section. It says this line only reports the problem. The real fix is fetching more history, which belongs to whoever owns the protected workflow. The one lever this script has, shrinking the window, was deliberately not used because it would change what every count means.

Checks: --test all passed, cargo fmt --check, build, clippy (-D warnings) and cargo test passed, and tests/harness_logic.sh reported 157 passed, 0 failed.

Process problem you should know about: in this checkout, several of my edit_file calls reported success but the changes never reached the file. I caught it with grep, redid those edits with Python, and checked each one before committing. I don't know the cause. The final commit contains every change.
